  "marked": false,
  "answer": "<p>The most suitable choice is <b>Leverage AWS CloudFormation by creating Nested Stack Templates, assigning each logical layer to its own child stack within the deployment.</b> CloudFormation is purpose-built for automated infrastructure management, allowing each resource and change to be defined in code, tracked, and versioned through GitHub. Nested stacks provide logical separation, increased modularity, and fine-grained control over updates.</p><p><i>Use AWS OpsWorks Stacks to design three separate layers, modeling each logical tier of your stack accordingly.</i> is not ideal here because OpsWorks focuses on configuration management and lifecycle, but does not provide the same level of visibility, auditability, or template-based version control as CloudFormation. It also ties you to Chef or Puppet for configuration, which may not meet your auditing or tracking needs.</p><p><i>Rely on AWS Config to define a configuration package that AWS can apply across your cloud deployment.</i> is incorrect since AWS Config is intended for monitoring and compliance auditing, not for declarative infrastructure automation or orchestration.</p><p><i>Utilize Elastic Beanstalk Linked Applications, transmitting required DNS data between levels via the metadata interface.</i> is designed for application deployment and scaling rather than managing complex infrastructure layers or providing detailed version control of infrastructure definitions.</p><ul><li><b>Tip:</b> CloudFormation templates (and nested stacks) can be committed and tracked in GitHub, ensuring a complete version history and reproducibility for infrastructure changes.</li><li>Choose nested stacks for modular, layered architectures to simplify management and updates.</li></ul>",
  "query": "<p>Your organization is planning to automate deployment for three distinct logical layers within a large-scale cloud architecture. It's essential that all changes are tracked for audit and versioning, and you must tightly manage and document any updates over time. Deployment code should be maintained and versioned within a GitHub repository. Which approach should you use to automate this stack most effectively?</p>",
